          %h3#cyclic_equivariance
            Cyclic (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mi D
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi SO
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_D \hookrightarrow SO(2)
            \-)equivariance
          %p
            The global equivariant sphere spectrum for all the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cyclic+groups"} cyclic groups
            over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+group"} circle group
            is canonically a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cyclotomic+spectrum"} cyclotomic spectrum
            and as such is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+unit"} tensor unit
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} monoidal (infinity,1)-category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cyclotomic+spectra"} cyclotomic spectra
            (see there).
          %h3#equivariance_2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mi ADE
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi SO
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 3
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G_{ADE} \hookrightarrow SO(3)
            \-equivariance
          %p
            See at
            %em
              %a{:href => "quaternionic+Hopf+fibration#ClassInEquivariantStableHomotopyTheory"} quaternionic Hopf fibration – Class in equivariant stable homotopy theory
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+theory"} cohomology theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brown+representability+theorem"} represented
                by the equivariant sphere spectrum is
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+stable+cohomotopy"} equivariant stable cohomotopy
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sphere+spectrum"} sphere spectrum
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-periodic+sphere+spectrum"} 2-periodic sphere spectrum
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %p General lecture notes include
          %ul
            %li#Schwede15
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stefan+Schwede"}> Stefan Schwede
              , example 2.10 in
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lectures+on+Equivariant+Stable+Homotopy+Theory"} Lectures on Equivariant Stable Homotopy Theory
          %p
            Discussion in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rational+equivariant+stable+homotopy+theory"} rational equivariant stable homotopy theory
            includes
          %ul
            %li#GreenleesMay95
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Greenlees"}> John Greenlees
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+May"} Peter May
              = succeed "," do
                %em Generalized Tate cohomology
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.rochester.edu/people/faculty/doug/otherpapers/GM-Tate-543.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            The sphere spectrum in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+equivariant+homotopy+theory"} global equivariant homotopy theory
            is discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Rognes"}> John Rognes
                ,
                %em Galois extensions of structured ring spectra
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0502183"}> arXiv:math/0502183
                )
            %li
              %p
                Markus Hausmann, Dominik Ostermayr,
                %em Filtrations of global equivariant K-theory
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1510.04011"}> arXiv:1510.04011
                )
          %h3#relation_to_equivariant_framed_bordism Relation to equivariant framed bordism
          %p
            Proof that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+framed+bordism+homology+theory"} equivariant framed bordism homology theory
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brown+representability+theorem"} co-represented
            by the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+sphere+spectrum"} equivariant sphere spectrum
          %ul
            %li
              Czes Kosniowski,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Equivariant Stable Homotopy and Framed Bordism
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.jstor.org/stable/1997591"} jstor:1997591
          %h3#equivariance_3
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}/2
            \-equivariance
          %p
            Discussion of
            = succeed "-equivariant" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
